1. Goal: Someone visiting the page should understand your hobby and why you like it.

Requirements:

* One main heading with the hobby's name.
* A short introduction.
* Two sections, each with its own heading: "Why I enjoy it" and "How to get started."
* A list of three things a beginner needs.
* One relevant image with alternative text describing it.
* A link to a useful website about the hobby, with descriptive clickable text.

Skills you'll practice: Choosing HTML elements, organizing headings, and adding images, lists, and links. Include the basic HTML document structure if you know it.

Optional bonus: Make the link open in a new tab.
You're finished when: The page opens in your browser, the image appears, and the link works.
2.
* Give the main heading a colour of your choice.
* Give the page a background colour using the body selector.
* Bonus: Make the image smaller using the CSS width property. Try a value in pixels, such as 300px.
3.
Requirements: Add these properties to your existing body rule:

* font-family: try Arial, sans-serif.
* line-height: try 1.6 to increase the space between lines.
* padding: try 20px to add space between the content and the page edges.

Concepts: Typography and padding—space inside an element, between its edge and its content.

Bonus: Try a lighter background colour and compare readability.
4.
Your next challenge: make your two sections look like cards.

* Give both <section> elements the same class, named card.
* Write one CSS rule that gives those cards a white background and 20px of padding.
* Add margin-bottom: 16px; to separate the cards.

New concept: Padding adds space inside an element; margin 
Bonus: Try border-radius: 12px; for rounded corners.
5.
Next challenge: Your cards can stretch too wide on a large screen. Make both cards:

* No wider than 600 pixels.
* Centred horizontally.
* Able to shrink on narrower screens.
6.
Next challenge: turn your tennis page into a finished beginner’s guide.

Keep your current content and cards, then:

* Centre the main heading, introduction, image, and final link so they line up with the cards.
* Choose a colour scheme with readable text and a background you like.
* Add a third card titled “My favourite tennis shot,” with a short explanation.
* Give all section headings a consistent style using one CSS rule.
* Make the image fit on narrow screens without stretching or losing its proportions.
* Add a footer with a short “Made by…” credit.
* Make the link visibly change when a pointer hovers over it.

Skills: Selectors, shared styles, spacing, page alignment, responsive images, and hover states.

Optional bonus: Make the image’s corners rounded and give the cards a subtle shadow.

Finished means: All three cards match, the page looks organised, and nothing causes horizontal scrolling in a narrow preview.